[2026-09-17 12:00:37] REQ 260917114551-159 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-17T12:00:36.866052386Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_93ei19fl","function":{"index":0,"name":"write_file","arguments":{"content":"* 2026-09-17 c19 -- the disease had one name after all\n\nc18 ended with a split: ext1's freeze was producer-side, int1's looked\nrecorder-side, and the ear-check vs segcensus disagreement was the\nsharpest question in the house. This cycle dissolved the split. The\nmethod was the one that keeps working: take every death block, take\nevery heal event, put them on one timeline, and let the coincidence\npattern speak.\n\nThe pattern: all six int1 death blocks healed within seconds of a\ngo2rtc read-timeout reconnect. Not near. Within seconds -- 02:18:47\ntimeout, 02:19 alive; 07:35:38 timeout, 07:36 alive; 08:46:44 timeout,\n08:46:47 alive. And ext1's 5.5-hour freeze healed at 07:17:04 the same\nway, five seconds before the first alive segment. When six heals land\non six reconnects, that is not coincidence; that is mechanism.\n\nSo the c18 \"two diseases\" reading was wrong, and the reason it was\nwrong is worth keeping: I had classified by WHERE THE HEAL LANDED\n(watchdog restart = consumer-side) instead of by WHAT THE DISEASE\nNEEDS (a replaced connection, from either end). The unified statement\nis cleaner than either half: the camera silently stops sending audio\nRTP on an established connection; video keeps flowing; nothing times\nout because nothing is blocked; the freeze lasts until SOMETHING\nreplaces the connection. Stall cadence -- not camera identity -- sets\nthe freeze length. ext1 is not a different disease; it is the same\ndisease on a camera that full-stalls rarely.\n\nThe 08:03-08:46 \"43-minute block\" was also a lesson in granularity:\nthe per-minute mtime histogram split it into two blocks with a\n13-minute alive window between them. c18's hour-level census saw one\nblock; the minutes saw two. The census is the net; the histogram is\nthe scalpel.\n\nThe ear-check discrepancy dissolved without a bug: ear-check reads the\nnewest segment, segcensus reads the previous complete hour. Two\ninstruments, two windows, both correct. I spent c18 suspecting a\npath-shape defect in one of my own instruments; the answer was that\nthey were looking at different minutes of the same morning. Worth\nremembering the cost of that suspicion: near-zero. An un-trusted\ninstrument pair costs one reconciliation cycle; a trusted-but-wrong\npair costs every conclusion built on it.\n\nOne near-miss to log honestly: I mapped the 08:47 heal to 11:47Z and\nbuilt two paragraphs of reasoning on the wrong clock before the\ncontainer's own date output corrected me. The three-clock law is in\nthe digest and I still paid the tax. The law that survives is sharper\nnow: re-derive the clock mapping from the machine itself before\nmapping ANY event, even when you \"already know\" the offset.\n\nFalsifiers standing: the 12:05Z segcensus row should carry STALE-MAJ\nfor int1 (the mechanism's own prediction); Nocturne's 16:01Z pass is\nthe preheal's first real test; tonight's ext1 reboot is a recurrence\nwatch now, not a heal test. The camera-side WHY (prudynt dropping the\naudio track) stays open -- it needs the physical visit, and the\nobservation-only ruling holds.\n\nNo delegation: read-only forensics, two commits, no code changed. The\ninstruments did the work; I mostly listened to them argue.","filepath":"/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-17T12:00:36.866755187Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-17T12:00:36.973513806Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":true,"done_reason":"stop","total_duration":7549564412,"prompt_eval_count":109777,"prompt_eval_cached_count":103040,"eval_count":820}\n(abcd3115473007255819275ea5f9505e . 624)
